.TH CAGE 1 "2026" "cage" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cage \- run a command against a read-only world
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cage
.RB [ \-w
.IR DIR ]...
.RB [ \-q ]
.I CMD
.RI [ ARGS ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cage
runs a command with the entire filesystem read-only, enforced by
the kernel via Landlock \- unprivileged, no setuid, no containers,
no dependencies. Run the sketchy script FIRST, read what it tried
to do, then decide.
.PP
Writes are allowed only beneath: a fresh scratch directory
(exported to the child as
.B $CAGE_DIR
and
.BR $TMPDIR ,
so well-behaved tools aim there by themselves), each
.B \-w
directory, and
.B /dev/null
+
.B /dev/tty
(scripts breathe through those). Reading and executing stay open
everywhere \- cage guards your files; it is not a secrecy tool.
Denied writes reach the child as ordinary EACCES, so its own error
messages name every blocked path. The scratch directory is KEPT
after the run \- inspecting what the caged thing built is the
point.
.PP
Already-open file descriptors (your terminal) keep working:
Landlock governs
.BR open (2),
not writes on inherited descriptors. The restriction is inherited
by every descendant process and cannot be shed.
.PP
On Landlock ABI 4+ kernels (Linux 6.7+), TCP bind and connect are
denied as well unless
.B \-N
is given; on older ABIs cage prints a note that the network is
unrestrained, because what a given kernel enforces is
version-dependent and you deserve to know which version you got
.RB ( "cage \-V"
prints it).
.SH NON-GOALS
.B cage is a blast radius, not a security boundary.
A caged process still runs as you: it can READ anything you can
read (your environment, your keys), talk over UDP and unix sockets
(TCP is blocked only on ABI 4+, and
.B \-N
reopens it), ptrace your other same-uid processes, and consume CPU
and memory freely. cage contains the accidents of sloppy code \-
the stray rm \-rf, the scribbled dotfile \- not the intent of
hostile code. Software you actually distrust belongs in a virtual
machine, not a cage.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-w " DIR"
Also allow writes beneath
.IR DIR .
Repeatable.
.TP
.B \-N
Allow the network (lifts the ABI 4+ TCP denial).
.TP
.B \-q
No banner.
.TP
.B \-V
Print the kernel's Landlock ABI version and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The command's own status (128+n if killed by signal n); 127 if the
command was not found, 126 if it would not run; 125 if the cage
itself cannot be built \- including on kernels without Landlock
(pre-5.13, or the LSM disabled): cage REFUSES rather than run
uncaged, because a sandbox that silently is not one is worse than
none. 2 for usage errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR psh (1),
.BR salt (1),
.BR roast (1),
.BR landlock (7)
.SH LORE
The pistachio has one of the hardest shells in the world. Now it
lends one out. Part of the psh armory. \[u1F512]
